INT. ALLEY LANES BOWLING - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

1950s. A table under a hanging green lamp. Smoke curls up through the cone of light. Through a swinging door, the muffled crash of pins.

DOMMY ESPOSITO, 50, silk vest, pomaded hair, sits at the head of the table dealing a patience hand. At his right, BIG LOU DEVEREAUX, 40s, a mountain in an overcoat, looks at nothing. Opposite them, FINGERS MCAVOY, 30s, nervous, a bandaged hand in his lap. TILLIE KOWALCZYK, 50s, bookkeeper, ledger open, sips cold coffee.

JUNE ABERNATHY-SOTO, 20s, a waitress in a pink uniform, sets a tray of beers on the sideboard and tries to leave unnoticed.

DOMMY
Stay a second, June.

JUNE
Mr. Esposito, I have the four lanes.

DOMMY
Four lanes can wait on a lady.

She stays. Stands very still.

TILLIE
The ledger's off by two thousand, Dommy.

DOMMY
I know, Tillie.

TILLIE
That's not a rounding error.

DOMMY
I know.

He turns a card. Red queen. He puts it on a black king.

DOMMY (CONT'D)
Fingers. How's the hand?

FINGERS
It's getting better. Doc says six weeks.

DOMMY
Who did it?

FINGERS
A door.

DOMMY
A door.

FINGERS
Slammed on it. In the garage.

BIG LOU
(low)
He's lying, boss.

FINGERS
Lou, come on.

BIG LOU
You went to the track Tuesday. Sandoval's boys picked you up.

The phone on the sideboard RINGS. Everyone looks at it. It rings again.

DOMMY
June. Get that.

She picks it up with two fingers.

JUNE
Alley Lanes.

SANDOVAL (O.S.)
(through the receiver, tinny)
Put Dommy on.

JUNE
Mr. Esposito, it's a Mr. Sandoval.

Dommy takes the receiver, keeps his eyes on Fingers.

DOMMY
Ray. Good evening.

SANDOVAL (O.S.)
Dommy. Your boy owes me four.

DOMMY
Four thousand?

SANDOVAL (O.S.)
Four thousand and a hand's worth of apology.

DOMMY
You took the hand already.

SANDOVAL (O.S.)
I took the hand. I want the four.

Dommy hangs up, gently. Looks at Tillie. At Big Lou. At Fingers, who shrinks.

DOMMY
Tillie. The two thousand that's missing.

TILLIE
Yes?

DOMMY
It's in Ray Sandoval's pocket.

Fingers puts his good hand over his face. Big Lou stands slowly. Dommy raises one finger and Big Lou sits again.

DOMMY (CONT'D)
Sit, Lou. We're a family here. June, honey, bring Fingers a whiskey.

June pours a shot with a trembling hand and sets it in front of him. He doesn't touch it.

DOMMY (CONT'D)
Drink. It's an order.

Fingers drinks. Dommy turns the next card.

DOMMY (CONT'D)
Tomorrow you go pay Ray what you owe, out of your own pocket, over the next ten years. And you thank him.

FINGERS
Yes, boss.

DOMMY
Tillie, fix the books.

TILLIE
It'll take till Friday.

DOMMY
Take till Friday.

Out in the lanes, a ball thunders. Ten pins fall at once. A cheer goes up, far off, innocent.

Dommy places the last card.

DOMMY (CONT'D)
Game's out.
